  "query_preview": "/Thursday through Monday/...\nWidespread showers will be the story Thursday into Friday, as \nthe low lifts northeast over our area from California. While \ncloud cover will help limit daytime instability, cooling aloft \nwill allow for weak instability to develop, resulting in a \nslight chance of embedded thunderstorms. Any thunderstorms will \nresult in localized heavier rainfall rates/totals underneath the\ncore. At least for Thursday, southerly flow aloft will keep \nsnow levels on the higher side (9-10kft MSL), giving rain its \nopportunity to shine as the dominate precipitation type. Over \nthe course of Thursday through Friday, guidance continues to \ntrend upwards on total accumulations, with lower elevations \nranging from 0.15-0.50 inches (lowest in eastern Oregon), \nmountain valleys ranging from 0.50-0.80 inches, and highest \nelevations up to 1.25 inches; with the bulk of the precipitation\nfalling late Thursday into early Friday. While this will be \nmore stratiform and long duration in nature, steep terrain/burn \nscars may experience increased runoff and land movement. \n\nA cold front on Friday will bring a rather sharp end to the\nprecip and a cooldown as it moves across our area from west to\neast. Temperatures comes Friday will drop around 5 degrees \nbelow normal. Another, probably more notable, consequence of the\ncold front will be the gusty west-northwest winds that will \naccompany it. Friday afternoon, winds across ridge tops and open\nterrain will gust to 20- 35 mph. The strongest winds, however, \nwill blow in the Snake River Plain east of Mountain Home where \ngusts will reach 35-45 mph. Lowering snow levels on Friday \nbehind the front will allow for a transition to snow/wintry mix \nfor elevations above 6.5 kft MSL. Although this switch will be \nat the tail end of the precipitation, so only a dusting of snow \nis expected along high elevation passes such as Banner Summit. \n\nPattern uncertainty increases behind this system, however\nSaturday at the very least should be dry with temperatures \nwarming to near normal again. While ensemble consensus is \nanother trough digging down to the west coast come Sunday, there\nis considerable uncertainty in how this low interacts with the \ndeparting on and when/if it get brought inland over our area.",
